@startuml 03-validation-conversion
!include _theme.iuml

title Two pipelines that get confused for one — validate() takes raw Python in; convert() moves an existing value between cty types

skinparam ConditionEndStyle hline
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11

partition "**validate()** — a raw Python object becomes a CtyValue" {
start
:a raw Python object —
dict, list, str, Decimal,
or an existing CtyValue;
:**CtyType.validate(value)**;

if (does this type recurse?) then (**no**: bool, number,\nstring, capsule)
  :**@preserves_marks**
  one frame, spent once, at the
  bottom of the descent; <<#DCE9F6>>
else (**yes**: list, set, map,\nobject, tuple, dynamic)
  :**@with_recursion_detection**
  two frames per nesting level — and that
  number is published, because the advertised
  depth limit is derived from it; <<#FADFE5>>
  :get_recursion_context()
  //a thread-local RecursionContext//;
  if (validation_path empty?) then (yes — top-level call)
    :context.reset();
  else (no — we are inside a descent)
  endif
  :push a depth marker onto validation_path;
  if (does the guard allow this value?) then (no)
    :**stop the whole descent.**
    Return an unknown of the source type
    carrying collect_marks_deep(value); <<#F5D9D9>>
    stop
  else (yes)
  endif
  note right
    **The guard refuses for four reasons**, checked in
    this order — a nested call has already stopped;
    every 64th call, elapsed > max_validation_time_ms;
    depth > max_depth_allowed; or id(value) has been
    seen more than max_object_revisits times, which is
    a circular reference. Primitives and flat sequences
    of them skip the cycle counter — it is not free,
    and neither can close a cycle.
    ----
    **Every refusal still carries the marks.** Stopping is
    exactly when a value must not lose its sensitivity:
    the caller gets an unknown either way, and an
    unmarked unknown is the silent declassification the
    whole mechanism exists to prevent.
  end note
endif

:run the type's own validate body;
note right
  A collection validates each element through its
  **element_type**; an object through its **attribute_types**;
  a tuple positionally. CtyDynamic infers a concrete
  type first and then delegates to //that// type's
  validate — the one extra guard entry that
  DYNAMIC_DELEGATION_RESERVE exists to pay for.
end note

if (a RecursionError escaped?) then (yes)
  if (did the stack run out inside pyvider.cty's own frames,\n//and// is validation_path at least MIN_OWNED_OVERFLOW_DEPTH?) then (no)
    :**re-raise.** Someone else's overflow is a broken
    input, and turning it into an unknown would make it
    indistinguishable from a legitimately undecided value; <<#F5D9D9>>
    kill
  else (yes — this descent is what exhausted the stack)
    :degrade to an unknown carrying the deep marks; <<#F5D9D9>>
  endif
else (no)
endif

:**reapply_marks(source, result)**
the rule stated once: if the input was a marked
CtyValue, the output carries the same marks; <<#D9EDE1>>
:pop the depth marker (in a finally); <<#E7E9EE>>
:a **CtyValue** of this type;
stop
}

partition "**convert()** and **unify()** — moving between cty types" {
start
:**convert(value, target_type)**
//wrapped in an error_boundary recording source type,//
//target type, nullness and unknown-ness//;

if (value.type equals target,\nor target with its optional\nattributes stripped?) then (yes)
  :return the value untouched — optionality is a
  property of a type //constraint//, not of a value; <<#D9EDE1>>
  stop
else (no)
endif

if (is the value null or unknown?) then (yes)
  if (can_convert_unsafe(source, target)?) then (no)
    :**CtyConversionError.**
    Nullness is not part of a cty type, so a null
    list is no more a string than a populated one; <<#F5D9D9>>
    kill
  else (yes)
    :a null, or an unknown, **of the target type**; <<#D9EDE1>>
    stop
  endif
else (no — a known value)
endif

:**the conversion table, in this order**
1. target is a capsule with convert_to_fn — the
destination decides first, which only matters
when both ends are capsules
2. source is a capsule with convert_fn
3. source is dynamic → convert what it wraps
4. target is dynamic → the value, marks intact
5. to string: **from number or bool only**
6. to number: from string or number
7. to bool: from "true"/"1"/"false"/"0", exact case
8. collections, tuples, objects: element by element;

:carry the source's marks onto the result; <<#D9EDE1>>
:a **CtyValue** of the target type;
stop
}

partition "the two type-level questions convert() rests on" {
start
:**can_convert_unsafe(source, target) : bool**
//lru_cache(1024) — types only, no value//;
note right
  go-cty's GetConversionUnsafe as a predicate.
  **"Unsafe" is go-cty's word for a conversion that depends**
  **on the value as well as the type**: every string has a
  number type available to it, but only some strings are
  numbers. This library exposes the unsafe predicate
  only — there is no safe-only variant — so the
  safe/unsafe split shows up here as the difference
  between what the types permit and what convert()
  can actually pull off on the day.
end note
:**unify(types) : CtyType | None**
//lru_cache(1024), via compare_types + sort_types//;
note right
  Decides the element type of every set operation,
  of concat, of chunklist over a tuple, and of
  setproduct's tuple arguments — so unifying
  differently disagrees with go-cty across a whole
  family of functions at once.
  ----
  **None, not dynamic, for "no common type".** Dynamic is
  a real answer — it is what a group of collections
  containing a dynamic unifies to — so using it for
  failure made an error indistinguishable from a
  result. _unify_by_preference then asks
  can_convert_unsafe, which is why the two must
  agree exactly.
end note
stop
}

@enduml
